---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
---
<Base title="The documents"
      eyebrow="Four civil records, read for the first time"
      dek="The family tree carried these scans for years with nobody transcribing them. They are not snapshots — they are death registrations, a death notice and a marriage register. Reading them corrected three dates, killed one family legend, and caught the surname changing on a single sheet of paper.">

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1905 · The death registration that settles the biggest question</h2>
  <p class="dek">Cape Colony, <em>Form of Information of a Death, Act No. 7 of 1894</em>. Entry 299,
    sub-district Indwe, district Wodehouse. Registered 14 September 1905.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/death-1905-willem-hermanus.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Cape Colony death registration form for Willem Hermanus Booysen, 1905, handwritten in ink." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death registration of <strong>Willem Hermanus Booysen</strong>, Indwe, 1905.
      MyHeritage tree <em>Defranceski Family Site (23andMe)</em>, extracted 9 September 2026.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem">Usual place of residence</td><td><strong>Doorn Street, Indwe</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>Fifty Five years</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Mason</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Date of death</strong></td><td><strong>September Thirteenth 1905</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Intended place of burial</td><td>Public Cemetery, Indwe</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cause of death</strong></td><td><strong>Ulcer of Stomach</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Duration of last illness</td><td><strong>Fourteen months</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Medical man</td><td>W. C. Macdonald, M.B.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Informant</td><td><strong>J. B. Booyzen</strong> — qualification: <strong>Present at Death</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Total children</td><td>Twelve</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">What it proves</h3>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> The death date the tree flagged
        <em>"To be confirmed, not sure"</em> is <strong>correct</strong>. He died at Indwe on
        <strong>13 September 1905</strong>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>He was not shot by the
        British.</strong> He died of a <strong>stomach ulcer after fourteen months of illness</strong>,
        under a doctor's care, in his own house, three years after the war ended.
        <a href={u("/rebels/")}>What that does to the testimony &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A farmer who became a mason</h3>
      <p>At his marriage in 1879 his occupation is <strong>Boer</strong> — farmer. At his death in 1905 it
        is <strong>Mason</strong>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">A man who works land and then works stone has, in between, very often
        <strong>lost the land</strong>. The war sits in that interval.
        <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> Suggestive, not probative — but it is exactly the
        trajectory the burned-farm testimony describes.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The name changing, on one sheet of paper</h2>
  <p class="dek">The registering clerk writes the deceased's surname. Four lines below, his son signs his
    own. They are not the same word.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-clerk-booysen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Handwritten name on the 1905 form reading Willem Hermanus Booysen, with an s." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The clerk, September 1905: <strong>Willem Hermanus Boo<em>y</em>sen</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1905-signature-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Informant signature on the 1905 form reading J B Booyzen, with a clear z." />
      <figcaption class="cap">His son, the same day: <strong>J. B. Boo<em>y</em><em>z</em>en</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    The father is registered under the old spelling. The son signs the new one. <strong>Both are on the
    same page, in September 1905 — three years after the Anglo-Boer War ended.</strong>
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">The informant is <strong>John Barry Booyzen</strong> (b. 1884), the eldest
    son, then twenty-one, recorded as <em>present at the death</em>. This is as close to a photograph of
    the surname changing as the archive is ever likely to find.
    <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1879 · The marriage register — and one ambiguous letter</h2>
  <p class="dek"><em>Duplicaat Origineel Register</em>, in Dutch. Entry 15, 22 December 1879. Married in
    the <strong>Nederduits Gereformeerde Kerk at Barkly</strong>, after banns, by <strong>David Ross</strong>,
    <em>consulent</em>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/marriage-1879-booysen-barry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dutch-language duplicate marriage register page for 1879, Barkly, Wodehouse district." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Marriage of Willem Hermanus Booysen and Catharine Mary Barry, Barkly, 1879.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Groom</th><th>Bride</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Name as entered</td><td><strong>Willem Hermanus Booysen</strong></td><td><strong>Catharine Mary Barry</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td class="m">22</td><td class="m">21</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Status</td><td>Jongman</td><td>Jongedochter</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Rank or occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Boer</strong> (farmer)</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td><strong>Lady Grey</strong></td><td><strong>Barkly</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><strong>Witnesses: J. Sutherland and P. Schoeman.</strong> Set that beside
    Anna Johanna Maria <strong>Schoemann</strong> marrying Hendrik Nicholaas Barry in 1877, the 1901
    Pretorius–Barry wedding held <em>at the home of D. Schoeman, "Caerlaverock", Barkly East</em>, and a
    <strong>Commandant D. Schoeman</strong> leading Cape Rebels at Labuschagne's Nek in 1900. The Schoemans
    are braided through this family across three generations.
    <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:32px">The most consequential letter in the archive</h3>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0 0;max-width:520px">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1879-groom-signature.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Extreme magnification of the groom's 1879 signature surname, reading Booij-something-en." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The groom's own signature, 1879, at maximum available magnification.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>The clerk wrote <b>Booysen</b>. The groom signed what reads as <b>"Booij_en"</b> — using the older
      Dutch <b>ij</b> digraph, with a medial letter whose descending loop is more consistent with
      <b>z</b> than <b>s</b>.</p>
    <blockquote>
      If that reading is right, <strong>he was already signing with a z in 1879</strong> — twenty years
      before the Anglo-Boer War — and the testimony's explanation of <em>why</em> the spelling changed is
      a later rationalisation of something that had already happened.
    </blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip inf">Not established</span> <b>The reading is not certain.</b> Cursive Dutch
      <em>s</em> and <em>z</em> are genuinely confusable, and this is a photograph of a duplicate register,
      not the original. The archive states both readings and prefers neither.</p>
    <p><b>This single letter is now the most consequential unresolved question here.</b> It wants an eye
      trained on nineteenth-century Cape Dutch hands, and the original register at the Cape Archives.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1915 · John Barry's death notice — typewritten, and therefore unarguable</h2>
  <p class="dek">Prepared by solicitors at Barkly East, stamped 18 April 1915, No. 925. Signed by
    <strong>James H. Barry, "Son (second)"</strong>. <b>This is the very document the family's earlier
    research note describes</b> — it can now be quoted rather than paraphrased.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1915-john-barry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Typewritten 1915 death notice for John Barry, Barkly East." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of John Barry, died 1903, notice prepared 1915.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:15rem"><strong>Birthplace and nationality</strong></td>
            <td><strong>"At sea (exact whereabouts unknown), British."</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Father</td><td>John Barry (address unknown)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Mother</strong></td><td><strong>Barry, born Neagle</strong> (Christian names and address unknown)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>83 years (about)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Occupation</td><td><strong>Carpenter and builder</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td><strong>Manorowen</strong>, Barkly East District</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Spouse</td><td>Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry, born <strong>Kolbe</strong> (died 2 August 1912). In community.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place of last marriage</td><td><strong>"Burghersdorp (probably)"</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Day of decease</strong></td><td><strong>3rd August 1903</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where died</td><td>on farm <strong>Manorowen</strong>, district Barkly East</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Estate</td><td>movable <strong>Yes</strong> · immovable <strong>No</strong> · exceeds £300 <strong>Yes</strong> · will <strong>No</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">"At sea" is official, not folklore</h3>
      <p>The Irish/High-Seas ambiguity is <strong>contemporaneous and legal</strong>, sworn by his own son.
        The mother's maiden name <strong>Neagle</strong> — almost certainly <strong>Nagle</strong>, of
        County Cork — is on a state document, not a family memory.
        <a href={u("/barry/")}>The Barrys &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Manorowen is Welsh</h3>
      <p>The tree renders the farm "Manor Owen", which hides it. The notice types it as one word:
        <strong>Manorowen</strong> — <strong>a village in Pembrokeshire, Wales</strong>. He also owned
        <strong>no immovable property</strong>: he lived on Manorowen but did not own it. Both are new and
        entirely unexplored.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>Two corrections fall out of this page.</b><br />
    <b>1. The death date is 3 August 1903</b>, not the 25 August the tree carries — a 22-day discrepancy.<br />
    <b>2. A duplicate person is resolved.</b> The tree lists both <em>Catherine Maria Sophia "Kathleen"
    Barry (1859–1940), married Booyzen</em> and a separate sister <em>Kathleen Mary Barry (Booysen)</em>
    with no dates. This notice lists <b>only one</b> Barry daughter married to a Boyson. The arithmetic
    closes exactly: the tree's <b>11</b> children = the notice's <b>8</b> surviving + <b>2</b> who
    predeceased him + <b>1 duplicate</b>. They are the same woman, and the archive's earlier claim of
    "two Barry sisters marrying Booysens" is <b>withdrawn</b>.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1947 · George's death notice corrects his own death date</h2>
  <p class="dek">Signed by <strong>G. Booyzen</strong> at Johannesburg on 21 February 1948 under the
    Administration of Estates Act 1913.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1947-george.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Handwritten 1947 death notice for George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen, Johannesburg." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of <strong>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen</strong>, died
      11 December 1947 at Forest Hill, Johannesburg.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1947-death-date.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail of the 1947 death notice showing the day of decease as 11th December 1947." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"The day of the decease: On <strong>11th December 1947</strong>" — died at
      <strong>85 Rheeden St, Forest Hill</strong>, Johannesburg.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    <strong>He died on 11 December 1947 — not 21 February 1947.</strong><br />
    21 February is the date the notice was <em>signed</em>, and it was signed in <strong>1948</strong>.
    A transcriber conflated the two and lost ten months.
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">This also dissolves an anomaly this archive had itself flagged: a burial at
    West Park recorded on <b>14 December 1947</b> looked like a ten-month delay. It was not.
    <b>He died on the 11th and was buried on the 14th — three days later</b>, exactly as one would expect.</p>
  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1947-occupation.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail showing age 46 years 6 months and occupation S.A.R. and H." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Age <strong>46 years 6 months</strong>; occupation
      <strong>S.A.R. &amp; H.</strong> — the South African Railways and Harbours; residence
      <strong>85 Rheeden Street, Forest Hill</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1947-spouses.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail showing surviving spouse Gertruida Alexandra born Kinnear and pre-deceased spouse Anna Johanna Maria born Pretorius." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Surviving spouse <strong>Gertruida Alexandra Booyzen, born Kinnear</strong>, in
      community of property; pre-deceased spouse <strong>Anna Johanna Maria Booyzen (Pretorius), died 26
      February 1944</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1947-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail listing the children of the prior marriage with birth dates for the minors." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Children of the prior marriage. The two eldest are entered as majors; the four
      minors carry birth dates — <strong>25/3/29, 12/10/30, 22/9/32, 10/1/34</strong> — which
      <strong>match the tree exactly</strong>, independently confirming Catherine Mary Sophia's birth on
      25 March 1929. The second daughter is given a married name the tree does not have:
      <strong>Kelly</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>He worked for the railways, not the mines.</b> The testimony's "the boys all got jobs on the mines"
    belongs to an earlier generation. By the 1940s this branch was <b>S.A.R. &amp; H.</b>
    <a href={u("/johannesburg/")}>Johannesburg &rarr;</a><br /><br />
    The notice also records <b>"no children of last marriage"</b> — consistent with both infant Gertrude
    Elizabeths having died before him.
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The photographs</h2>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0;max-width:560px">
    <img src={u("/docs/photo-george-isaak-transkei.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Two men shaking hands in open grassland, one young in a 1930s suit, one elderly with a white moustache and walking stick." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"George Booyzen and Isaak Pretorius In Transkei"</strong> — the tree's
      own caption. Left: <strong>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen</strong>, in his thirties, in the wide
      trousers of the 1930s. Right: <strong>Isaak Pretorius</strong>, a relative of George's wife, walking
      stick planted in the grass behind him. <strong>This appears to be the only photograph of George in
      the tree</strong> — his profile still carries MyHeritage's prompt to add a personal photo.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Three uncaptioned photographs</h3>
  <p class="dek">Attached to George's profile with no caption, no date and no tagging. The identifications
    below are <span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> — reasoning is given so it can be overturned.</p>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/photo-untitled-43.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="A young woman in a patterned dress, an elderly woman seated in black, and a young man outside a house." />
      <figcaption class="cap">An <strong>elderly woman seated in full black</strong> between two young
        people, outside a plastered house. <em>Hypothesis:</em> the seated woman is
        <strong>Kathleen Barry (1859–1940)</strong> in widow's black — the dress and print date the image
        to the 1930s, when she was in her seventies and living in Johannesburg. <strong>Unverified.</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/photo-untitled-45.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="A young man in a dark uniform tunic and peaked cap standing in a yard by a bare tree." />
      <figcaption class="cap">A young man in a <strong>dark uniform tunic and peaked cap</strong>.
        <em>Hypothesis:</em> <strong>George Downing Mountjoy Booyzen in South African Railways and
        Harbours uniform</strong> — the employer his 1947 death notice names.
        <strong>Unverified.</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/photo-untitled-46.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="A man in a light suit and fedora leaning on a late-1920s sedan with a small boy beside him." />
      <figcaption class="cap">A man in a light suit and fedora on a late-1920s sedan, registration
        <strong class="m">TAS 486</strong>, a small boy beside him. The registration is a hard,
        researchable detail. <strong>Unidentified.</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1819 · A sworn falsehood in Shoreditch</h2>
  <p class="dek">A marriage allegation of the <strong>London Diocese</strong>, 20 January 1819 — the sworn
    statement required before a marriage licence could be issued. Fee, 2/6.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-marriage-allegation-1819.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Handwritten 1819 marriage allegation from the London Diocese for George Kolbe and Margaret Downing." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Marriage allegation of George Kolbe and Margaret Downing, London Diocese,
      20 January 1819, signed <strong>G. Kolbe</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    "Appeared personally <strong>George Kolbe</strong> and made Oath, that he is of the Parish of
    <strong>Saint Leonard Shoreditch</strong> in the County of Middlesex, <strong>a Bachelor of the age of
    Twenty-one Years and upwards</strong>, and intendeth to marry with <strong>Margaret Downing</strong> of
    the same Parish, <strong>a Spinster of the age of Twenty-one Years and upwards</strong>…"
  </blockquote>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-allegation-age-detail.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Magnified detail of the oath reading a Bachelor of the age of Twenty-one Years and upwards." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"…a Bachelor of the age of <strong>Twenty-one Years and upwards</strong>."</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>Both ages are false, and the document is an oath.</b><br />
    George Augustus Kolbe was born <b>7 December 1802</b>. On 20 January 1819 he was
    <b>sixteen years and six weeks old</b>. Margaret Downing was born <b>23 February 1803</b>; she was
    <b>fifteen years and eleven months</b>.<br /><br />
    Under <b>Hardwicke's Marriage Act of 1753</b> a minor under twenty-one could not marry by licence
    without a parent's consent. Swearing to be "of the age of twenty-one years and upwards" was the
    ordinary way round it — and it was perjury. The four weeks' residence in the parish was very often
    nominal too.
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    This archive's 1820 Settler <strong>married at sixteen, by lying under oath about his age</strong>,
    and sailed for Africa eleven months later. <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a>
  </blockquote>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1844 · The estate the archive had a reference for but had never read</h2>
  <p class="dek">The Cape Archives reference <span class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/35: 7698 (1845)</span> was
    already noted here as "not yet ordered". It was in the family tree all along — the estate number
    <strong>7698</strong> is written at the head of the page.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:820px">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-death-notice-1844-zoom.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1845 death notice for George Augustus Kolbe under Ordinance 104, estate number 7698." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of <strong>George Augustus Kolbe</strong>, made under Section 9,
      Ordinance 104, at Colesberg in January 1845 and signed <strong>M. Kolbe</strong> — Margaret, his
      widow.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem">Birth-place</td><td><strong>London</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Names of the parents</td><td><em>left blank</em></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>42 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Condition in life</strong></td><td><strong>Agriculturist</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Day of the decease</td><td>1 December 1844</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where died</td><td><strong>At Knapdaar, in the field cornetcy of Rhenosterberg, District of Colesberg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Property</td><td><strong>"about £1000 Sterling"</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">He died a farmer</h3>
      <p>The tree gives his occupations as <b>Reverend Missionary of the London Missionary Society</b>,
        <b>Chemist</b> and <b>Tailor</b>. The official record at the end of his life calls him simply an
        <b>agriculturist</b>, and he died on his own farm. Those are not contradictions — they are a life
        in sequence — but the archive should stop leading with "missionary".</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Ten children, and one who is missing</h3>
      <p>The notice names ten. <b>Keetor Peter Benjamin Kolbe</b>, whom the tree gives a birth in 1840, is
        <b>not among them</b> — nor among the ten in his mother's 1867 notice. He died young, or he does
        not belong in this family. <b>Julia Kolbe</b>, whom MyHeritage flags as born two years after her
        father's death, is likewise absent, as she must be.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1867 · Margaret's death notice solves a problem this archive had raised</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/downing-death-notice-1867.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1867 death notice for Margaret Downing, widow of George Augustus Kolbe, Burghersdorp." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice of <strong>Margaret Downing, widow of the late George Augustus
      Kolbe</strong>, estate 4094, filed 30 January 1868 and signed by her son-in-law
      <strong>G. D. O. Werdmuller</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem">Birthplace</td><td>London</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Names of the parents</strong></td><td><strong>"Both dead"</strong> — no names given</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Age</strong></td><td><strong>64 years 9 months &amp; 2 days</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Day of the decease</td><td>25 November 1867</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Where died</td><td>at her own residence in <strong>Burghersdorp</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Property</td><td><strong>a dwelling house in Burghersdorp and a farm in the division of Albert</strong>; household furniture, live stock, sheep and cattle</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><b>"64 years 9 months &amp; 2 days"</b> on 25 November 1867 computes to a
    birth of <b>23 February 1803</b> — exactly what the tree says and exactly what the gravestone says.
    A three-way agreement, which in this archive is rare enough to be worth stating.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>And it settles the Enslin problem.</b> This archive flagged an impossibility: Margaretha Kolbe
    appearing to marry <b>George Frederick Enslin</b> in 1850 while married to John Augustus Barry, and
    bearing Enslin and Barry children <b>three and a half months apart</b> in 1851. The archive's own
    guess was that the Enslin children belonged to her <b>sister Mary Elizabeth</b>.<br /><br />
    The notice lists, among Margaret Downing's children:
    <b>"Mary Elizabeth (dead) who was married to George F. Enslin — left issue of 6 minor children."</b>
    <span class="chip doc">Resolved</span> The Enslin marriage and its children are
    <b>Mary Elizabeth's, not Margaretha's</b>. The tree has attached one sister's family to the other.
    <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">One thing it does <em>not</em> give: her parents, entered only as
    <strong>"Both dead"</strong>. The tree's <strong>John Paul Downing</strong> and
    <strong>Mary P King</strong> are therefore not corroborated here.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The grave at Burgersdorp</h2>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-grave-monument.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="A tall urn-topped granite monument inside a cast-iron railed enclosure in a grassy cemetery." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The monument, inside its cast-iron enclosure, Burgersdorp Old Cemetery.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-grave-inscription.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Close view of the cut inscription on the Kolbe monument." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The inscription.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    SACRED / TO THE MEMORY OF / <strong>GEORGE AUGUSTUS KOLBE</strong>, / BORN IN LONDON 7 DECEMBER 1802, /
    AND DIED AT HIS FARM <strong>WURTHEMBURG</strong>, / DIVISION OF ALBERT, 1 DECEMBER 1844. /
    ALSO HIS RELICT / <strong>MARGARET DOWNING</strong>, / BORN IN LONDON 23 FEBRUARY 1803, /
    AND DIED AT BURGHERSDORP / 25 NOVEMBER 1867.
  </blockquote>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:20px">
    <p>The farm is spelled <b>WURTHEMBURG</b> in cut stone, with a <em>th</em> — closer to
      <em>Württemberg</em> than the tree's "Wurtemburg". And the stone says <b>"HIS FARM"</b>: he owned it,
      which Margaret's 1867 notice confirms with "a farm in the division of Albert".</p>
    <p>She is commemorated under her <b>maiden name</b>, as <em>his relict</em> — the old word for widow.
      She outlived him by twenty-three years and was buried in his grave, as the Dutch note on the
      MyHeritage record says: <em>"Zijn vrouw is later bijgezet in het graf."</em></p>
    <p class="cap">The 1845 notice says <em>district of Colesberg</em>; the stone says
      <em>division of Albert</em>. Both are right — Albert, with its seat at Burgersdorp, was carved out
      of the older district afterwards. The stone is the later text.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The portraits — and one that cannot be who it says</h2>
  <div class="grid g3" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-couple-misattributed.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Carte-de-visite of a seated bearded man and a standing woman in a bustle dress." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Captioned "Margaret Elizabeth Georgina Downing and George Kolbe"
        — and it is not them.</strong> See below.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/downing-margaret-portrait.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Heavily retouched head-and-shoulders portrait of a woman with centre-parted hair and a white collar." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>Margaret Downing.</strong> Heavily hand-retouched, but the centre
        parting and plain white collar fit the <strong>1850s–60s</strong>, which fits a woman who died in
        1867. <span class="chip inf">Plausible</span></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-george-portrait.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Vignetted portrait of a young man with dark curling hair and a high collar." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>George Augustus Kolbe.</strong> He died in <strong>1844</strong>,
        when photography was five years old and vanishingly rare in the Cape interior. If it is him, it is
        a copy of a <strong>painted miniature</strong>. <span class="chip inf">Unverified</span></figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The couple photograph is misattributed.</b> It is a <b>carte-de-visite</b> — albumen print on a card
    mount with rounded corners. The woman wears a cuirass bodice with a long buttoned front, a ruched
    overskirt and a <b>bustle</b>; the man has a full beard and a watch chain. That costume is
    <b>c. 1875–1885</b>.<br /><br />
    <b>George Augustus Kolbe died in 1844. Margaret Downing died in 1867.</b> Neither can appear in a
    photograph taken in the late 1870s. A pencilled inscription on the mount does not appear to read
    "Kolbe".<br /><br />
    <span class="chip inf">Hypothesis</span> It is their son <b>George Augustus Kolbe (b. 1836)</b> — who
    would be about forty in 1876, matching the sitter — with his wife. The repeated forename is the
    obvious route by which a caption goes astray. <b>Unproved.</b>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:26px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/st-annes-soho.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="St Anne's church, Soho, Westminster." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>St Anne's, Soho</strong> — where he was christened, 2 February 1803.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/st-leonards-shoreditch.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async" alt="St Leonard's church, Shoreditch." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>St Leonard's, Shoreditch</strong> — where he swore the oath and
        married, 20 January 1819.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1842 · The will of a Bond Street tailor</h2>
  <p class="dek">A <strong>Prerogative Court of Canterbury</strong> register copy, from the Public Record
    Office. Signed <strong>25 May 1842</strong>; proved at London <strong>23 February 1846</strong>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-1.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Blackletter heading reading This is the last Will and Testament, with a marginal note naming John Gottlob Kolbe." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"<strong>This is the last Will and Testament</strong> of me
      <strong>John Gottlob Kolbe of Conduit Street, Bond Street</strong>, in the County…"</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-2.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dense register-hand page of the will naming trustees and the partnership." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"…of Middlesex <strong>Tailor</strong> I give devise and bequeath all my
      Estate both Real and Personal … unto <strong>Timothy Duke</strong> of Lombard Street, Gentleman,
      <strong>Christian William Kent</strong> of Bentinck Street, Poland Street, Tailor, and
      <strong>my daughter Marian Kolbe</strong> … <strong>upon Trust</strong>."</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:26px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">He had a partner and a going concern</h3>
      <p>"…an agreement with my present partner <b>Mr Richard Davies</b> that the Executors … should
        permit the capital to be invested by me in our <b>partnership business of Tailors</b> at the time
        of my death <b>to remain in such business</b>…" — for a term of <b>fourteen years</b> after he
        died.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">This is not a jobbing tailor. It is a firm with capital, a partner, and
        a fourteen-year plan.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">His daughter was an executrix</h3>
      <p>He appointed <b>Timothy Duke, Christian William Kent and Marian Kolbe</b> — his own daughter —
        <b>"Executors and Executrix"</b>. At probate she is sworn as
        <b>"Marian Kolbe Spinster the daughter."</b></p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">A woman administering a substantial estate in her own right in 1846 is
        worth pausing on.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>And a provision with a sting in it.</b> For those fourteen years the profits were to be divided
    among such of his daughters <b>"as shall continue sole and unmarried"</b> — and a daughter's share
    <b>ceased entirely if she married</b>, and was added to her unmarried sisters'. A maintenance fund for
    spinster daughters, and an incentive not to leave.
  </div>

  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-3.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Page of the will setting out the residue clause and division among children." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The residue: "…<strong>unto for and amongst all and every my child and
      children who may be living at the time of my decease in equal shares</strong> … and
      <strong>the issue of such of my children respectively as may be dead</strong>, such issue however to
      take only the share to which their parent would have been entitled."</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    <strong>George Augustus Kolbe died on 1 December 1844. His father died on 18 November 1845 — a year
    later.</strong> Under that clause, George's share passed <strong>to his own children</strong> — to
    Margaret Downing's family at Burgersdorp, and so to <strong>Margaretha Kolbe, who married John
    Augustus Barry</strong>.
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:14px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> <b>There is, on paper, a line of money
    from a Bond Street tailor's estate to the Barkly East family.</b> A later clause names
    <b>"my said children or grandchildren"</b> explicitly. Whether a shilling of it ever reached the Cape
    is unknown and untested — it would show in the PCC estate papers and in the Cape estate files.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>

  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-will-5.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Final page of the will with signature, witnesses and the probate act." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Signed <strong>"J. G. Kolbe"</strong>, 25 May 1842, "written in one sheet of
      paper". Witnessed by <strong>Wm Gribble Jr</strong> and another, described as
      <strong>"Clerks to Mr Duke, Lombard Street"</strong> — the executor's own clerks.
      <strong>PROVED at London the 23rd February 1846</strong> before Herbert Thomas Pratt, Doctor of Laws
      and Surrogate.</figcaption>
  </figure>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1845 · Two documents correct the tree twice over</h2>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-death-certificate-1845.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1845 General Register Office death certificate for John Gottlob Kolbe, May Fair district." />
    <figcaption class="cap">General Register Office, entry <strong>206</strong>, district of
      <strong>May Fair</strong> within <strong>St George Hanover Square</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:13rem">When died</td><td><strong>Eighteenth of November 1845, at 34 Conduit Street</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age</td><td>80 years</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rank or profession</td><td><strong>Tailor</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cause of death</strong></td><td><strong>"Decay of Nature"</strong> (certified) — the Victorian phrase for old age</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Informant</td><td><strong>S. Leather, in attendance, 34 Conduit Street</strong> — an attendant, not family</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Correction 1 — he did not die in Lamb's Conduit Street</h3>
  <p class="dek">The tree records <em>"34 Lamb's Conduit Street"</em>. Three independent documents say
    otherwise.</p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:16px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:16rem">Document</th><th>Address as given</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Death certificate, 1845</td><td><strong>34 Conduit Street</strong>, registered at <strong>May Fair</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Burial register, 1845</td><td><strong>"Conduit Street, Regent Street"</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>His own will, 1842</td><td><strong>"of Conduit Street, Bond Street, in the County of Middlesex, Tailor"</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:18px"><b>Conduit Street</b> runs between Regent Street and New Bond Street, in
    <b>Mayfair</b>. <b>Lamb's Conduit Street</b> is in <b>Bloomsbury</b>, over a mile away and in the
    <b>Holborn</b> registration district — a death there could not have been registered at May Fair.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px;align-items:start">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The consequence</h3>
      <p>The photograph in the tree captioned <b>"34 Lamb's Conduit Street"</b> is
        <b>a picture of the wrong building, in the wrong part of London</b>. Someone went and
        photographed a real 34 — just not his.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>He lived and died above his own shop, in Mayfair.</b></p>
    </div>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-wrong-building.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Modern photograph of a shopfront reading Langham Gallery, with doors numbered 34 and 34A." />
      <figcaption class="cap">34 Lamb's Conduit Street, Bloomsbury — <strong>not</strong> the Kolbe
        address.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>

  <h3 style="margin-top:36px">Correction 2 — he was not buried in Warwickshire</h3>
  <p class="dek">The tree records <em>"St John the Baptist, Leamington, Warwickshire."</em></p>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-burial-heading.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Register heading reading Burials in the Parish of St John Baptist in the German Savoy Church in the County of Middlesex." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The register's own heading: <strong>"BURIALS in the Parish of St John Baptist,
      in the GERMAN SAVOY CHURCH, in the County of Middlesex."</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-burial-detail.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Burial register entry number 55 for Mr John Gottlob Kolbe of Conduit Street, Regent Street." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>No. 55</strong>: <strong>Mr John Gottlob Kolbe</strong>, abode
      <strong>Conduit Street, Regent Street</strong>; died 18 Nov, <strong>buried 26 November 1845</strong>,
      aged <strong>81</strong>; ceremony by <strong>Revd Dr Steinkopff</strong>, <strong>Grave 7,
      Rows 1–2</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    Somebody matched the dedication <em>St John the Baptist</em> to the parish church at
    <b>Leamington Spa</b>. It is the <b>German Lutheran Chapel Royal of the Savoy</b>, in the
    <b>Strand</b> — a hundred miles away, and the <b>same congregation whose children's burial ground
    already holds five of his children and his wife Mary Larman</b>.<br /><br />
    <b>The correction makes the family more coherent, not less.</b> They are German Lutherans of the Savoy
    from first to last. The officiant, <b>Revd Dr Steinkopff</b>, is Karl Friedrich Adolf Steinkopf
    (1773–1859), minister of the German Savoy Church and a founder of the British and Foreign Bible
    Society. <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">The neighbouring entries in the register are almost all German —
    Wüst, Nitsch, Wuleko, Stürcke, Kollmann, Schnitzlein, Grubusch, Wettig. A German congregation in
    London, burying its own.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1901 · A wedding in a rebel's house, under martial law</h2>
  <p class="dek">The archive knew from a note in the tree that this marriage was held "at the home of
    D Schoeman". The original register gives the man a <strong>signature</strong>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/marriage-1901-caerlaverock.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dutch-language duplicate marriage register for 1901, Caerlaverock, Barkly East district." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Marriage of <strong>Petrus Jacobus Pretorius</strong> and
      <strong>Magrita Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry</strong>, 8 July 1901. Book 4584, entry 5.
      The card in the frame is the South African Department of Home Affairs' damage notice.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Groom</th><th>Bride</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:12rem">Age</td><td class="m">22</td><td class="m">21</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Veeboer</strong> — stock farmer</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Residence</strong></td><td><strong>“Arundale”</strong> (reading uncertain), Barkly Oost</td><td><strong>Caerlaverock</strong>, Barkly Oost</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    "Getrouwd in <strong>woonhuis D. Schoeman</strong> te <strong>Caerlaverock</strong> voormeld, na
    huwelijksgeboden, door mij: <strong>J. G. van der Walt, Pred. Geref. Kerk</strong>."
  </blockquote>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:620px">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1901-witnesses.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Two witness signatures reading J. J. Parsons and D. W. Schoeman." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The witnesses: <strong>J. J. Parsons</strong> and
      <strong>D. W. Schoeman</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The initials are new, and they matter.</b> In <b>March 1900</b>, about <b>400 Cape Rebels under
    Commandant D. Schoeman</b> held <b>Labuschagne's Nek</b>, north of Dordrecht — the next district over.
    Sixteen months later a <b>D. W. Schoeman of Caerlaverock, Barkly East</b> signs this register as a
    witness, in his own house, under martial law.<br /><br />
    <span class="chip inf">Hypothesis</span> That is now a <b>specific, searchable identity</b> to set
    against the rebel commandant lists — not the bare initial the archive had before.
    <b>It is still not proof.</b> <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Married in a house, not a church</h3>
      <p>In the middle of the war, in a private dwelling. And in the <b>Gereformeerde Kerk</b> — the
        "Dopper" church — not the <em>Nederduitse</em> Gereformeerde Kerk in which Willem Hermanus Booysen
        had married in 1879. A different, more conservative denomination.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And two more British farm names</h3>
      <p>He was of a farm whose name reads as <b>“Arundale”</b> — the word is genuinely ambiguous at full magnification — and she of <b>Caerlaverock</b>. Set beside John Barry's
        <b>Manorowen</b>, the farms of this district carry <b>Scottish and Welsh</b> place-names —
        Dumfriesshire, Scotland, Pembrokeshire.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> These are British settlers' farms,
        and the families on them were siding with the republics. That complicates the picture in a way
        worth keeping.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>c. 1909 · Anna Pretorius, aged about four</h2>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/photo-pretorius-family-c1909.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Studio portrait of a man, a woman, a boy of about seven and a small girl in a white dress." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Captioned in the tree
        <strong>"anna johanna maria pretorius with brother and parents"</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p><b>Left:</b> a boy of about seven in a pleated tunic and wide collar — <b>Dirk Jacobus
        Pretorius</b> (b. 8 June 1902).<br />
        <b>Centre:</b> <b>Petrus Jacobus Pretorius</b> (1878–1941), the stock farmer.<br />
        <b>Right:</b> <b>Margarietha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Barry</b> (1880–1961), in a high-necked blouse
        with her hair dressed in a pompadour.<br />
        <b>Front:</b> a small girl of about four — <b>Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius</b> (b. 3 Oct 1905).</p>
      <p><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> The hairstyle and blouse date the picture to about
        <b>1908–1912</b>; the children's ages narrow it to roughly <b>1909</b>.</p>
      <p>This is <b>David Defranceski's great-grandmother as a small child</b> — the woman who would die at
        thirty-eight of an ectopic pregnancy at Forest Hill, leaving Catherine orphaned at eighteen. It
        appears to be the only photograph of her parents together, eight years after they married in
        D. W. Schoeman's house.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1940 · Kathleen Barry's death certificate, and one unexpected word</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:860px">
    <img src={u("/docs/kathleen-barry-death-1940.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1940 South African death form for Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen, born Barry." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><em>Form of Information of a Death / Aangifte van 'n Sterfgeval</em>,
      Act No. 17 of 1923. Entry <strong>5970</strong>, district Johannesburg.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:15rem">Name</td><td><strong>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen</strong> — "(Born Barry.)"</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Age · birthplace · condition</td><td>82 · <strong>Cape Province</strong> · <strong>Widow</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Occupation</td><td><strong>Home Duties</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Pensioner</strong></td><td><strong>MILITARY PENSIONER</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Place of death</strong></td><td><strong>No. 1, Fifteenth Avenue, Mayfair, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Intended place of burial</strong></td><td><strong>Brixton Cemetery, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Causes of death</td><td><strong>Carcinoma of the Uterus</strong>; Senility — <strong>one year one month</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Informant</td><td><strong>M. v. Rensburg</strong>, "Causing Burial", of 20 Commercial Road, <strong>Fordsburg</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1940-military-pensioner.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail of the death form reading Occupation Home Duties, Pensioner or dependent of pensioner: Military Pensioner." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"8. Occupation — <strong>Home Duties</strong>. 9. Pensioner or dependent of
      pensioner — <strong>Military Pensioner</strong>."</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>An eighty-one-year-old widow in Mayfair, drawing a military pension in 1940.</b> Her husband died in
    1905 of a stomach ulcer, so it is not his service.<br /><br />
    <span class="chip inf">Untested</span> The likeliest route is a <b>son's First World War service</b> —
    several of her sons were of age — with a dependant's pension attaching to her. It is a hard,
    searchable fact and <b>an entirely new thread in this archive</b>.<br /><br />
    It also carries an irony worth leaving in rather than smoothing away: <b>a family that remembers
    fighting the British ends with its matriarch on a military pension in a British dominion.</b>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:20px">Two smaller gains. The archive had only "Mayfair" for her death; it now has
    <b>No. 1, Fifteenth Avenue</b>. And the tree records <b>no burial place for her at all</b> — she is at
    <b>Brixton Cemetery</b>. <a href={u("/johannesburg/")}>Johannesburg &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1912 · "Father: Kolbe, thinks James was christian name"</h2>
  <p class="dek">Of everything read in these four days, this is the line that most justifies the archive
    existing.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:900px">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-barry-death-notice-1912.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1915 death notice for Margaret Elizabeth Barry, born Kolbe, of Manorowen, Barkly East." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Death Notice for <strong>Margaret Elizabeth Barry, born Kolbe</strong>,
      who died 2 August 1912 in the house of her son at <strong>Manorowen</strong>. Signed
      <strong>R. P. Barry</strong>, <em>"Son in whose house deceased died"</em>, Barkly East, 13 May 1915.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <figure style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1912-father-unknown.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Detail of the form: Father — Kolbe, thinks James was christian name. Mother — Unknown." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"3. Names and addresses of the Parents of the Deceased —
      <strong>Father: Kolbe, thinks James was christian name. Mother: Unknown.</strong>"</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p>Her son, filling in his own mother's death notice, <b>did not know his grandfather's name</b>.
      He guessed <em>James</em>. Of his grandmother he knew nothing at all.</p>
    <p>His grandfather was <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b> — who married at sixteen on a sworn lie in
      Shoreditch, sailed on the <em>Nautilus</em> in 1819, preached across the Orange River, named a Karoo
      farm after his father's Württemberg, and lies under a cut-stone monument at <b>Burgersdorp</b> with
      his name, his London birth and his farm carved into it. His grandmother was <b>Margaret Downing</b>
      of London, buried in the same grave.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>The family forgot them inside seventy years, while the headstone stood forty miles up the
      road.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p>Two generations after the <em>Nautilus</em>, the notice describes her as
      <b>"Afrikander of European extraction"</b> and guesses her birthplace: <em>"Believe Colesberg
      District."</em> <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>And she died with nothing.</b> Movable property: <em>No</em>. Immovable: <em>No</em>. Estate:
    <em>Nil</em>. No will. She died in her son's house at Manorowen.<br /><br />
    Set that beside her mother <b>Margaret Downing</b>, who in 1867 left a house in Burgersdorp and a farm
    in the division of Albert; beside her grandfather <b>Johan Gottlieb Kolbe</b>, whose 1842 will
    disposed of a partnership in a Bond Street tailoring firm; and beside her husband <b>John Barry</b>,
    whose estate in 1903 exceeded £300. <b>The Kolbe money did not last three generations.</b>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">This notice also supplies a <strong>third</strong> date for John
    Barry's death — <strong>"August 1904"</strong> — against the <strong>3 August 1903</strong> of his own
    1915 death notice and the <strong>25 August 1903</strong> in the tree. Both notices were made in 1915,
    by two different sons, and they contradict each other.
    <a href={u("/disputed/")}>Disputed &rarr;</a></p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1923 · The Schoemans get a name</h2>
  <p class="dek">The archive has been chasing a "D. Schoeman" of Caerlaverock since the beginning. This
    document does not name him — but it plants his family.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0;max-width:760px">
    <img src={u("/docs/schoeman-barry-sterfkennis-1923.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1923 Sterfkennis for Anna Johanna Maria Barry, born Schoeman, of Evaton." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><em>Unie van Zuid-Afrika — Sterfkennis</em> for
      <strong>Anna Johanna Maria Barry, née Schoeman</strong>, died 24 February 1923 at
      <strong>Evaton, district Potchefstroom</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:13rem">Geboorteplaats</td><td><strong>Queenstown</strong>, Cape Province</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Vader</strong></td><td><strong>Ruben Schoeman</strong>, <strong>Barkly Oost</strong>, K. Prov.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Moeder</strong></td><td><strong>Magdalena Johanna Botha</strong>, Barkly Oost, K. Prov.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Echtgenoot</td><td><strong>Hendrik Nicolaas Barry</strong>, in community of property</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Plaats van laatste huwelik</td><td><strong>Barkly Oost</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Signed</td><td><strong>D. Booyzen</strong>, <em>"dochter van overledene"</em>, present at the death</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Ruben Schoeman of Barkly Oost</h3>
      <p>Anna Johanna Maria Schoemann — who married Hendrik Nicholaas Barry in 1877, and whose grandchild
        Anna married George Booyzen in 1924 — was the daughter of <b>Ruben Schoeman</b> and
        <b>Magdalena Johanna Botha</b>, <b>both of Barkly Oost</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">The naming confirms it: her fourth son is
        <b>Ruben Jacobus Josuah Barry</b>, after his maternal grandfather.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> This does not identify
        <b>D. W. Schoeman</b> of Caerlaverock — but it places a named Schoeman family in exactly the right
        district and generation to hold him. <a href={u("/rebels/")}>The rebels &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And a Booyzen signs it</h3>
      <p>The informant is <b>"D. Booyzen"</b> — <b>Dorothea Regina Barry</b>, who married
        <b>Henry James Booyzen</b> at Elliot in 1912. She signs as a Booyzen, daughter of the deceased,
        present at the death.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">A third document, independent of the other two, confirming the third
        Barry–Booyzen marriage. <a href={u("/cousins/")}>The cousin marriage &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1803 · The London end of the line, closed</h2>
  <p class="dek">A baptism register index from <strong>St Anne's, Soho</strong> — birth date at the left,
    day of baptism at the right. A note on the same page records that the register holds
    <em>"410 Baptisms for the Year 1802."</em></p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1803-baptism-entry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Register entry under the heading February 1803 reading 1802 Dec 7 George Augustus Kolbe of John Gottlob and Mary, 2." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Under <strong>FEBRUARY 1803</strong>, the second entry:
      <strong>"1802 Dec: 7 — George Augustus Kolbe of John Gottlob &amp; Mary — 2"</strong></figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p>Born <b>7 December 1802</b>. Baptised <b>2 February 1803</b>. Son of <b>John Gottlob and Mary</b>.</p>
    <blockquote>
      An English parish register and a Karoo gravestone, written ninety years and six thousand miles
      apart, give the same birth date to the day: <strong>7 December 1802</strong>.
    </blockquote>
    <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> And the parentage is no longer taken on the tree's word.
      The link from <b>George Augustus Kolbe</b>, the 1820 Settler, to <b>John Gottlob Kolbe</b>, tailor
      of Conduit Street, and <b>Mary Larman</b> is <b>on the parish register</b>.
      <b>The London end of this line is closed.</b></p>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-baptism-register-1803.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Full two-page baptism register index for December 1802 to May 1803." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The full opening, December 1802 to May 1803. Westminster parish records,
      FindMyPast reference <span class="m">GBPRS_WSMTN_005109304_00342</span>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>And a small correction of emphasis.</b> The register writes the father <b>"John Gottlob"</b>. So
    does his <b>will</b> — <em>"of me John Gottlob Kolbe"</em>. So does his <b>death certificate</b>. So
    does his <b>burial register</b> — <em>"Mr John Gottlob Kolbe"</em>.<br /><br />
    <b>Four independent documents, and not one of them says "Johan Gottlieb."</b> That is the German
    original the family tree leads with. <b>"John Gottlob" is the name he actually used, and was buried
    under, in England</b> — and this archive now follows the documents.
    <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1906 · A one-digit slip, caught by two documents</h2>
  <p class="dek">A <strong>Gereformeerde Kerk</strong> baptism register. Entry 29, under
    <em>Gedoopt 11 Nov. 1906</em>:</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1906-anna-baptism.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Baptism register entry reading Anna Johanna Maria, geb. 3 Oct. 1906, Petrus Jacobus Pretorius, Margaretha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Berry, ouders." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><strong>"29. Anna Johanna Maria, geb. 3 Oct. 1906 — Petrus Jacobus Pretorius,
      Margaretha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Berry } ouders."</strong> The birth year is written identically to
      the <em>1906</em> in the line above it.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p>The tree gives her birth as <b>3 October 1905</b> and her christening as <b>11 November 1906</b> —
      a thirteen-month gap. The register shows the two are <b>five weeks apart</b>, both in 1906. It is
      the same register the christening date came from; the birth year was mistranscribed by one digit.</p>
    <p>And her own death form settles it independently: <b>"Age — 37 years"</b> at 26 February 1944.
      A birth in October 1905 would make her 38.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <strong>Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius was born
      3 October 1906.</strong> A contemporary baptism register and a death registration agree, against
      the tree.
    </blockquote>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:26px 0 0;max-width:760px">
    <img src={u("/docs/anna-death-form-1944.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1944 death form for Anna Johanna Maria Booysen, born Pretorius." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>2045</strong>, Johannesburg. Note the surname typed
      <strong>Booysen</strong>, with an <em>s</em>, in 1944.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem">Age</td><td><strong>37 years</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place of death</td><td><strong>85, Rheeders Street, Forrest Hill, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Intended place of burial</strong></td><td><strong>West Park Cemetery</strong> — the same ground as her husband; the tree gives only "Johannesburg"</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Causes of death</strong></td><td><strong>"Haemorrhage due to Ruptured Tubal Pregnancy (extra-uterine). Not 1st child."</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Informant</td><td>"Causing Burial", of <strong>9–11 Jack Street, Germiston</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1961 · Margarietha Barry, and a pension that is only ordinary</h2>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0;max-width:700px">
    <img src={u("/docs/margarietha-death-form-1961.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Typewritten 1961 death form for Margerietha Wilhelmina Elizabeth Pretorius, born Barry." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>180/61</strong>, district Germiston.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:15rem"><strong>Age and date of birth</strong></td><td><strong>81 years, 16.3.1880</strong> — confirming the tree exactly</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place of birth</td><td><strong>Barclay East, Cape Province</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Died</td><td><strong>First April 1961, General Hospital, Germiston</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Causes of death</td><td><strong>Uraemia — Hypertensive Congestive Cardiac Failure</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Residence</td><td>5 Plaza Court, Rietfontein Road, <strong>Primrose, Germiston</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Burial</td><td><strong>Primrose Cemetery</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Allowance</td><td><strong>Old Age</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Identity number</td><td><strong>"Unobtainable"</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>The contrast is the point.</b> Margarietha Barry, dying in 1961, drew an <b>Old Age</b> allowance —
    the ordinary thing. Her husband's mother-in-law <b>Kathleen Barry</b>, dying in 1940, was a
    <b>Military Pensioner</b>. That really is exceptional, and it still has no explanation.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">One small human detail. The informant here, <strong>P. Gouws of
    9 Jack Street, Germiston</strong>, gives the same address as the informant on Anna's 1944 form
    seventeen years earlier. Almost certainly an <strong>undertaker's premises</strong> rather than a
    relative — a reminder that "informant" on these forms often means the funeral director, not family.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>Two corrections to this archive's own work</h2>
  <p class="dek">Both were found by reading the last four documents. Both were mine, not the tree's.</p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The farm was not "Armadale"</h3>
      <p>This archive read the groom's 1901 residence as <b>Armadale</b> and built a small argument on it —
        that the Barkly East farms carried <b>Scottish</b> names. At full magnification on the
        <em>original</em> register the word is <b>genuinely ambiguous</b>: it reads as <b>"Arundale"</b>,
        possibly <em>Armsdale</em> or <em>Ormsdale</em>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><b>"Armadale" was an over-reading and is withdrawn.</b> The wider point
        survives on the two names that <em>are</em> certain — <b>Caerlaverock</b> (Dumfriesshire) and
        <b>Manorowen</b> (Pembrokeshire) — but the archive should not have leant on a word it could not
        read.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Catherine was orphaned at eighteen</h3>
      <p>This archive said in four places that Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen was
        <b>"seventeen years old and had no parents."</b> That was calculated from the <em>wrong</em> death
        date for her father — the tree's 21 February 1947, which this archive itself later corrected to
        <b>11 December 1947</b> without carrying the correction through to her age.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Born 25 March 1929, she was <b>eighteen</b> when her father died, and
        <b>fourteen</b> when her mother did. <b>Corrected throughout.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1851 · The baptism that settles an age, and names a godfather</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-baptism-entry.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Dutch Reformed baptism register entry 2809 for Willem Hermanus, born 4 May 1851." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>No. 2809</strong>: <em>Het Kind</em>
      <strong>Willem Hermanus</strong>, <em>Geboren</em> <strong>4 Mei 1851</strong>, <em>Vader</em>
      <strong>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen</strong>, <em>Moeder</em>
      <strong>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</strong>, <em>Gedoopt</em>
      <strong>31 October 1851</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The age question is settled</h3>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Documented</span> <b>Born 4 May 1851</b>, from a contemporary register,
        matching the tree exactly.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">So of the three figures the archive had, <b>the marriage register is the
        outlier</b>: it gives his age as <b>22</b> in December 1879, when he was <b>28</b>. His 1905 death
        registration says <b>55</b>, which is right within a year. <b>The 1879 entry is wrong by six
        years.</b></p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And he was named for his godfather</h3>
      <p>The witnesses are <b>Willem Hermanus Pieterzen</b>, <b>Maria Catharina Magdalena Potgieter</b> and
        <b>Xaverius Hermanus Pieterzen</b>. <span class="cap">(The archive scan corrects this archive's
        earlier reading of the surname as <em>Pietersen</em> — see below; the first given name, earlier read
        as <em>Iwerus</em>, is still not certain.)</span></p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Johanna Catharina Mountjoy's <b>half-brothers were Pieterzens</b> — the
        sons of <b>Anna Catharina Sleer</b> by her first marriage. The archive had them only as names
        in a list. Here one of them is standing at the font, <b>and the child takes his name.</b>
        <a href={u("/women/")}>The women &rarr;</a></p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:20px">The register's place column reads <em>achter Sneeuwberg</em> — the family
    was farming <b>behind the Sneeuberg</b>, not living in Cradock town.</p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Resolved — by a better scan, and not in the direction expected</h3>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:14px">
    <p>This section previously read the father's surname here as <b>"Booij_en"</b>, with the same
      ambiguous medial letter as the 1879 signature, and concluded: <em>"I cannot separate them from an
      image&hellip; The change is real. Its date is not established, and <b>no scan will establish
      it.</b>"</em></p>
    <p>That was read from a <b>photograph of the register on the family tree</b>. The archive has since
      read the <b>Cape Town Archives scan of the register itself</b>, at nine times magnification. It is
      not ambiguous at all:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-father-booyzen.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1851 register, father's name reading Petrus Jacobus Booyzen with a clear z." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The father: <strong>Petrus Jacobus Boo<em>y</em><em>z</em>en</strong></figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/detail-1851-pieterzen-control.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1851 register witnesses, both reading Pieterzen with the same z letterform." />
      <figcaption class="cap">The witnesses, same hand, same ink:
        <strong>Pieter<em>z</em>en</strong>, twice</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:18px">
    <p><b>The letter is a z.</b> And it is a z for the Pieterzens too — a family with no name-change story
      attached to it. In Dutch orthography <b>-zen</b> is simply how the patronymic ending is written, and
      this clerk wrote it that way for everyone.</p>
    <blockquote>The scan settled the letter. Settling the letter is what took the argument away.</blockquote>
    <p><b>What this costs.</b> The z cannot have been <em>invented</em> by anyone in this family — it was
      ordinary Cape-Dutch spelling, sitting in the registers in 1851. The <b>1879 signature question is
      not merely unsettled but largely emptied</b>: a z-shaped letter in a Dutch hand is not evidence of
      intent.</p>
    <p><b>What survives, unchanged.</b> The <b>1905 death registration</b>, which turns on
      <em>two spellings of one man's name on one sheet</em> — clerk <b>Booysen</b>, son
      <b>Booyzen</b> — and not on the shape of any single letter. The tree's recorded
      <b>"Former name: Booysen"</b>. And the <b>distribution data</b>: 646 against 53,764, Gauteng against
      the Cape, gathered a century later by people who had never heard of this family.</p>
    <p><b>The change is real. The spelling it changed to was not new.</b>
      <a href={u("/name/")}>The name &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1946 and 1947 · The two Gertrudes — a contradiction resolved</h2>
  <p class="dek">This archive flagged as <em>Serious</em>: two daughters recorded born on
    <strong>11 May</strong> a year apart, both named Gertrude Elizabeth. Either two children given the
    same name and the same birthday — or one record duplicated a year out.</p>
  <p><b>Both certificates exist, and they are irreconcilably different documents.</b></p>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/gertrude-1-death-1946.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1946 death form for Gertrude Elizabeth Booyzen, aged 4 days." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>4256</strong>, registered 16 May 1946.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/gertrude-2-death-1947.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="1947 typewritten death form for Gertrude Booyzen, aged 6 hours." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>3920</strong>, registered 13 Mei 1947.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Gertrude Elizabeth Booyzen</th><th>Gertrude Booyzen</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:11rem">Date of death</td><td><strong>15 May 1946</strong></td><td><strong>11 May 1947</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Age</strong></td><td><strong>4 days</strong></td><td><strong>6 hours</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Place of death</strong></td><td><strong>Queen Victoria Hospital</strong></td><td><strong>Turf Club Nursing Home</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Cause</strong></td><td>Prematurity <strong>± 30 weeks</strong></td><td>Atelectasis; prematurity <strong>7½ months</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Doctor</td><td>Dr Dennis W. P. Lavery</td><td>J. S. Porter</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Burial</strong></td><td><strong>West Park Cemetery</strong></td><td><strong>Primrose Cemetery, Germiston</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:24px">
    Two hospitals. Two cemeteries. Two doctors. Two registration entries a year apart. Four days, and six
    hours. <strong>There were two children.</strong> <span class="chip doc">Resolved</span>
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:16px"><b>And the coincidence the archive doubted is real.</b> Both were born on
    <b>11 May</b>, exactly a year apart, and both were badly premature — the first at about thirty weeks,
    the second at seven and a half months. The first lived four days. The second lived six hours. They are
    not buried together.</p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    Set the whole sequence out, and it happened inside <b>three years and ten months</b>:<br /><br />
    <b>February 1944</b> — Anna dies at 85 Rheeder Street, aged 37. &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    <b>May 1946</b> — Gertrude Elizabeth, four days old. &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    <b>May 1947</b> — Gertrude, six hours old. &nbsp;·&nbsp;
    <b>December 1947</b> — George, aged 46.<br /><br />
    <b>Catherine Mary Sophia Booyzen was eighteen.</b> Two years later she married at St Joseph's,
    Mayfair. <a href={u("/johannesburg/")}>Johannesburg &rarr;</a>
  </div>
  <p class="cap" style="margin-top:18px">Three smaller corrections. The first Gertrude <strong>did not die
    at 85 Rheeden Street</strong> — the certificate gives Queen Victoria Hospital and names the street only
    as the mother's residence. The second child is registered simply <strong>"Gertrude"</strong>, not
    Gertrude Elizabeth. And the street appears as <strong>Rheeder</strong>, <strong>Rheeders</strong> and
    <strong>Rheeden</strong> across three documents.</p>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1819 · The marriage entry — and a bride who could not sign her name</h2>
  <p class="dek">The allegation was sworn that morning. This is the register entry itself,
    <strong>St Leonard's Shoreditch, No. 858</strong>.</p>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-marriage-entry-1819.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1819 marriage register entry for George Kolbe and Margaret Downing at St Leonard's Shoreditch." />
    <figcaption class="cap">"George Kolbe of this Parish, Bachelor, and Margaret Downing of this Parish,
      Spinster, were married in this Church <strong>by Licence</strong> … this <strong>Twentieth</strong>
      Day of <strong>January</strong> … <strong>Nineteen</strong>. By me <strong>Tho: Baxter,
      Min<sup>r</sup></strong>."</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="prose" style="margin-top:24px">
    <p>He signed <b>George Kolbe</b> — after striking through a first attempt. She did not sign.</p>
    <blockquote>
      <strong>Margaret Downing † her mark.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p><b>She could not write her own name.</b> She was fifteen.</p>
    <p>And in 1867 she died at Burgersdorp owning <b>a dwelling house and a farm in the division of
      Albert</b>, having buried her husband at forty-one and raised ten children across a mission circuit
      from Uitenhage to Bethulie. <b>It is the steepest arc in this archive</b>, and it starts with a
      cross in a Shoreditch register.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The blank that proves the lie</h3>
      <p>The line reading <b>"with Consent of ————"</b> is <b>left empty</b>. Under Hardwicke's Act a
        minor marrying by licence needed a named consenting parent. The blank is the visible consequence
        of the oath they had sworn that morning: that both were <em>"of the age of Twenty-one Years and
        upwards."</em> He was sixteen; she was fifteen.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">And a witness called "John Paul"</h3>
      <p>The witnesses are <b>John Paul</b> and <b>Mary Downing</b>. The tree records the groom's father,
        Johan Gottlieb Kolbe, as also known as <b>"John Paul" Kolbe</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px"><span class="chip inf">Inferred</span> If that is him, <b>the father
        witnessed the marriage</b> — which makes this something more complicated than an elopement: a
        parent standing by while his sixteen-year-old son swore he was of age. <b>Unproved</b>; "John
        Paul" could be an unrelated man.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>The portrait is a painting — as it had to be</h2>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:20px;align-items:start">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/kolbe-george-painted-portrait.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Painted portrait of a young man with reddish-brown curling hair, high white stock and tan coat." />
      <figcaption class="cap"><strong>George Augustus Kolbe</strong> — a painting, photographed under
        glass. The flare across the chest is the glazing.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>This archive had cautioned that any likeness of a man who died in <b>1844</b> — when photography
        was five years old and almost unknown in the Cape interior — would have to be
        <b>a painted portrait</b>, not a photograph from life.</p>
      <p><span class="chip doc">Resolved</span> <b>It is.</b> A young man with reddish-brown curling hair,
        clean-shaven, in a <b>high white stock</b> and a <b>tan coat with wide lapels</b> — dress of the
        <b>1820s and 30s</b>, which fits a man born in 1802.</p>
      <p class="cap">A third file in the same set is a left-facing profile so heavily processed —
        posterised, speckled, colour-fringed — that it is either a separate image or a modern
        "restoration" that has invented a great deal. <b>Not relied on here for likeness.</b></p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1924 · A tree nurseryman marries at Vereeniging</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/marriage-1924-vereeniging.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1924 duplicate original marriage register for George Downing Montjoy Booyzen and Anna Johanna Maria Pretorius." />
    <figcaption class="cap">No. <strong>15/24</strong>, solemnized at <strong>Vereeniging</strong>,
      district Heidelberg, by <strong>H. F. Steyn</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:24px">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Husband</th><th>Wife</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:11rem"><strong>Age</strong></td><td class="m">24</td><td class="m"><strong>18</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Boomkweker</strong> — tree nurseryman</td><td>Geen</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Residence</strong></td><td><strong>Evaton</strong></td><td><strong>Evaton</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Consent</td><td><em>Eigen</em> — his own</td><td><strong><em>Ouders</em> — her parents'</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:24px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">A third document for 1906</h3>
      <p>She is <b>18</b> at 30 December 1924 — which requires a birth in <b>late 1906</b>, not 1905. And
        her consent is recorded as <b>"Ouders"</b>, her parents' — required because she was a
        <b>minor</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">With the baptism register and the death form, <b>three independent
        documents now give 1906.</b> The tree is wrong three times over.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">Boomkweker</h3>
      <p>Not a miner, and not yet a railwayman. In 1924 <b>George grew trees.</b> A wholly new occupation
        for him — and by 1947 he was a <b>clerk on the South African Railways</b>.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">Both were living at <b>Evaton</b> — where Anna's grandmother
        <b>Anna Johanna Maria Schoeman</b> had died the year before. The whole connection had moved north
        together.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1947 · The death registration, and a garbled memory vindicated</h2>
  <figure style="margin:20px 0 0;max-width:680px">
    <img src={u("/docs/george-death-registration-1947.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1947 death registration for George Downing Mountjay Booyzen, entry 10,899." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Entry <strong>10,899</strong>, Johannesburg, registered 12 December 1947 —
      a <em>second</em> document, distinct from the death notice.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin-top:22px">
    <table>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td style="width:14rem"><strong>Occupation</strong></td><td><strong>Clerk, South African Railways, Johannesburg</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Date of death</strong></td><td><strong>Eleventh December 1947</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Place</td><td>85 Rheeders Street, Forest Hill · buried <strong>West Park</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Causes</strong></td><td><strong>Coronary Thrombosis; Chronic Myocarditis</strong></td></tr>
        <tr><td>Duration</td><td><strong>2 days / 11 months</strong></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:24px">
    <b>The 11 December date is now confirmed twice</b>, by two separate documents. The tree's 21 February
    1947 is wrong twice over.<br /><br />
    <b>And the tree's strangest note turns out to be substantially right.</b> It carried
    <em>"Cause: HEART ATTACK 1946"</em>, which read like nonsense against a February 1947 death. The
    registration gives <b>chronic myocarditis of eleven months' duration</b> ending in a
    <b>coronary thrombosis of two days</b>. A year-long heart illness, ending in a heart attack.
    <b>A garbled memory, but a true one.</b>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1889 · Two grandparents at a font, and a name on the facing page</h2>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1889-godparents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1889 baptism register entry 40 for Johanna Catharina, with godparents John Barry and Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth Kolby." />
    <figcaption class="cap"><em>Doop Register, Barkly Oost</em>, entry <strong>40</strong>, 15 September
      1889: <strong>Johanna Catharina</strong>, geb. 20 Juni 1889; <em>Ouders</em>
      <strong>Willem Hermanus Booijsen</strong> and <strong>Catharina Maria Sophia Barry</strong>;
      <em>Getuigen</em> <strong>John Barry</strong> and <strong>Margaret Wilhelmina Elizabeth
      Kolby</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <blockquote style="margin-top:22px">
    The godparents are the child's own <strong>maternal grandparents</strong> — <strong>John Augustus
    Barry I</strong>, the Irishman born at sea, and <strong>Margaretha Kolbe</strong>, the 1820 Settler's
    daughter, standing together at a font in Barkly East in September 1889.
  </blockquote>
  <p style="margin-top:16px">Her surname is written <b>"Kolby"</b> — a phonetic variant, twenty-six years
    before her own son would tell a magistrate he thought his grandfather's name was <em>James</em>.
    <a href={u("/kolbe/")}>The Kolbes &rarr;</a></p>

  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">And on the facing page, entirely by accident</h3>
  <figure style="margin:18px 0 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1889-mountjoy.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="1889 baptism entry for George, son of Hercules Johannes Mountjoy and Maria Sophia van Niekerk." />
    <figcaption class="cap">6 October 1889: <strong>George</strong>, geb. 21 Juli 1889; <em>Ouders</em>
      <strong>Hercules Johannes Mountjoy</strong> and Maria Sophia van Niekerk; among the witnesses
      <strong>Jakobus Jonathan Mountjoy</strong> and <strong>Maria Sophia Mountjoy</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <b>Mountjoys, alive and baptising at Barkly Oost in 1889.</b> This line descends from
    <b>Johanna Catharina Mountjoy</b> (1819–1863), and the archive had her family as little more than a
    surname and a father who died in 1827.<br /><br />
    <b>Hercules Johannes</b>, <b>Jakobus Jonathan</b> and <b>Maria Sophia Mountjoy</b> are a named,
    searchable cluster in the right district, twenty-six years after her death — and they turned up on the
    page opposite a Booyzen baptism, found by accident.
    <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
  <h2>1863 &middot; The death notice that adds a generation</h2>
  <p class="dek">Found by searching a spelling nobody had tried: <strong>Montjoy</strong>.</p>
  <p>
    The National Archives index had been searched for <b>MOUNTJOY</b> — forty-two documents, none of them
    the one wanted. Searched for <b>MONTJOY</b> it returns four, and two of them are the junction between
    the two families:
  </p>
  <div class="scroll" style="margin:18px 0">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>File</th><th class="m">Reference</th><th class="m">Year</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>MONTJOY, JAMES. DEATH NOTICE.</strong></td><td class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/21, 4393</td><td class="m">1840</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MONTJOY, JOHANNA CATHARINA. WIFE OF PETRUS JACOBUS BOOYSEN. WILL.</td><td class="m">KAB MOOC 7/1/268, 87</td><td class="m">1845</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>BOOYSEN, JOHANNA CATHARINA. (NEE MONTJOY). DEATH NOTICE.</strong></td><td class="m">KAB MOOC 6/9/104, 499</td><td class="m">1863</td></tr>
        <tr><td>MONTJOY, JOHANNA CATHARINA. LIQUIDATION AND DISTRIBUTION ACCOUNT.</td><td class="m">KAB MOOC 13/1/221, 6</td><td class="m">1866</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin:24px 0">
    <figure style="margin:0">
      <img src={u("/docs/death-notice-1863-johanna-catharina.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
           alt="Printed Cape death notice form of 1863 for Johanna Catharina Montjoy, completed in ink." />
      <figcaption class="cap">Filed <strong>25 July 1863</strong>, reference <strong>499</strong>.</figcaption>
    </figure>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The form, as completed</h3>
      <table style="width:100%">
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1. Name of the Deceased</td><td><b>Johanna Catharina Montjoy</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2. Birth-place</td><td><b>Graaff Reinet</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3. <b>Names of the Parents</b></td><td><b>James Montjoy and Anna Catharina Sleer</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>4. Age</td><td><b>44 years &amp; 19 days</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>5. Condition in life</td><td><b>farmer's wife</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>6. Married or unmarried</td><td><b>Married to Petrus Jacobus Booysen</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>7. Day of decease</td><td><b>19th May 1863</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>8. At what House</td><td><b>Zevenfontein, Brak River</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>10. Property</td><td><b>Moveable property</b></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
  <figure style="margin:22px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1863-parents.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line three of the 1863 death notice, reading James Montjoy and Anna Catharina Sleer." />
    <figcaption class="cap">Line 3 — the generation the archive did not have from any document:
      <strong>James Montjoy and Anna Catharina Sleer</strong>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <div class="grid g2" style="margin-top:22px">
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">An age given to the day</h3>
      <p>The form asks for years. The informant wrote <b>"44 years &amp; 19 days"</b>. Counted back from
        19 May 1863, that puts her birth at <strong>30 April 1819</strong>, at Graaff-Reinet.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">The tree had "1819". The document has the date, and the informant knew
        it without being asked.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="panel">
      <h3 style="margin-top:0">The surname, settled by convergence</h3>
      <p>The hand is genuinely ambiguous between <b>Sleer</b> and <b>Steer</b>, and this archive has
        over-read a single word before.</p>
      <p style="margin-top:12px">It is settled from outside: the family tree carries
        <b>Schleher</b>, FamilySearch's index carries <b>Slier</b>. Three renderings from three
        independent sources, <b>all with an l and none with a t</b>. The letter is an uncrossed
        <em>l</em>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">Eight children, and one of them is the direct line</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1863-children.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Line nine of the 1863 death notice listing eight children, one major and seven minors." />
    <figcaption class="cap">One <em>Major</em>, seven <em>Minors</em>.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Jacobus Nicholas &middot; Johanna Catharina &middot; Petrus Jacobus &middot; David Schalk &middot;
    <strong>Willem Hermanus</strong> &middot; Magdalena Johanna &middot; Jan Gerhardus Lodewicus &middot;
    Jacoba Catharina.
  </p>
  <div class="note">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>The fifth child is the man the 1905 death registration is about.</strong>
      <b>Willem Hermanus Booyzen</b>, baptised at Achter Sneeuwberg on 31 October 1851, twelve years old
      and a minor when his mother died. His parentage — <b>Petrus Jacobus Booyzen and Johanna Catharina
      Montjoy</b> — is now carried by two original registers that agree, and the Mountjoy descent enters
      this family through <em>his mother</em>.</p>
  </div>
  <p style="margin-top:20px">
    Seven of the eight are matched to baptisms in the Graaff-Reinet registers. An eighth child,
    <b>Anna Catharina</b>, baptised 21 September 1842, is <em>not</em> on the list — she died before her
    mother, and the notice is silent about her, as these forms are.
  </p>
  <h3 style="margin-top:34px">The signature</h3>
  <figure style="margin:16px 0;max-width:560px">
    <img src={u("/docs/detail-1863-signature.jpg")} loading="lazy" decoding="async"
         alt="Signature at the foot of the 1863 death notice reading P. J. Booy, an angular letter, then en." />
    <figcaption class="cap">The widower signs.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    <span class="chip inf">Not established</span> The surname ends in an angular letter with a descender —
    the form is more consistent with <b>z</b> than <b>s</b>, and the clerk who filled in the body of the
    form wrote <b>Booysen</b> at line 6. But the archive has just finished demonstrating, on the 1851
    register above, that <b>this letterform proves nothing on its own</b>. It is recorded here and
    claimed for nothing.
  </p>
  <div class="note" style="margin-top:22px">
    <p style="margin:0"><strong>Still to be read: MOOC 6/9/21, reference 4393 — the 1840 death notice of
      James Montjoy himself.</strong> It would name <em>his</em> parents, and with them the point at
      which the name entered the Cape. It is not name-indexed; the probate collection's 846 films are
      catalogued by film number alone, so reaching it means finding the film that covers the 1840 volume.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Open questions &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="blk">
